\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Passive and aggressive execution at nearly matched participation. ES futures, 246 sessions of calendar 2025, 100 contracts bought and 100 sold in every window, zero simulated latency on all three paths. Cost is in ticks per contract: a positive slippage is a loss, a negative relative cost is a gain. Intervals are 95\,\% and clustered by session, since windows within a day are not independent. \emph  {Crossed} is the share of executed quantity that filled on arrival rather than resting: it is the line that separates the two styles mechanically, and the cost lines agree across it.\relax }}{17}{table.caption.30}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:styles}{{3}{17}{Passive and aggressive execution at nearly matched participation. ES futures, 246 sessions of calendar 2025, 100 contracts bought and 100 sold in every window, zero simulated latency on all three paths. Cost is in ticks per contract: a positive slippage is a loss, a negative relative cost is a gain. Intervals are 95\,\% and clustered by session, since windows within a day are not independent. \emph {Crossed} is the share of executed quantity that filled on arrival rather than resting: it is the line that separates the two styles mechanically, and the cost lines agree across it.\relax }{table.caption.30}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Post-fill mark-outs cross-check.}{17}{section*.32}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Three measures of the same executions, in ticks per contract. \emph  {Relative slippage} prices the two legs of a window against the arrival mid and is a per-window quantity. \emph  {Mark-out} is the mid-price move over the stated horizon after each individual fill, one row per execution, signed so that positive is a move against the fill. Both are priced against the mid, so they are on the same scale and directly comparable. They measure \emph  {related} quantities rather than one quantity twice: relative slippage is the price a completed round trip achieved, mark-out is how the mid moved after each individual fill. Agreement between them is therefore a consistency check --- what a window paid lines up with what happened after its fills --- and not a replication. Both rows cover the full corpus: 245 sessions and $1.63$ million individual fills for the passive style, 246 sessions and $1.64$ million for the aggressive. Intervals are 95\,\% and clustered by session.\relax }}{17}{table.caption.33}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:markout}{{4}{17}{Three measures of the same executions, in ticks per contract. \emph {Relative slippage} prices the two legs of a window against the arrival mid and is a per-window quantity. \emph {Mark-out} is the mid-price move over the stated horizon after each individual fill, one row per execution, signed so that positive is a move against the fill. Both are priced against the mid, so they are on the same scale and directly comparable. They measure \emph {related} quantities rather than one quantity twice: relative slippage is the price a completed round trip achieved, mark-out is how the mid moved after each individual fill. Agreement between them is therefore a consistency check --- what a window paid lines up with what happened after its fills --- and not a replication. Both rows cover the full corpus: 245 sessions and $1.63$ million individual fills for the passive style, 246 sessions and $1.64$ million for the aggressive. Intervals are 95\,\% and clustered by session.\relax }{table.caption.33}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Post-fill mark-out for the two execution styles, at one, five and thirty seconds, with 95\,\% intervals clustered by session. The two are indistinguishable at every horizon, and each profile is flat rather than decaying: the price move is complete within a second of the fill and does not revert over the following twenty-nine. That is the signature of adverse selection rather than of temporary impact.\relax }}{18}{figure.caption.34}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:markout}{{1}{18}{Post-fill mark-out for the two execution styles, at one, five and thirty seconds, with 95\,\% intervals clustered by session. The two are indistinguishable at every horizon, and each profile is flat rather than decaying: the price move is complete within a second of the fill and does not revert over the following twenty-nine. That is the signature of adverse selection rather than of temporary impact.\relax }{figure.caption.34}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Window-level relative slippage for the two execution styles at matched participation, with 95\,\% intervals clustered by session. One style rests for 99\,\% of its executed quantity and the other crosses for half of it; the round trip costs the same either way.\relax }}{18}{figure.caption.35}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:slippage}{{2}{18}{Window-level relative slippage for the two execution styles at matched participation, with 95\,\% intervals clustered by session. One style rests for 99\,\% of its executed quantity and the other crosses for half of it; the round trip costs the same either way.\relax }{figure.caption.35}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Relative slippage against mark-out, both execution styles. The two quantities are computed from different data at different grains --- one from the volume-weighted prices of a whole window, the other from the mid-price after each individual fill --- so their agreement is a check on the measurement rather than an identity. They agree to within a couple of hundredths of a tick throughout.\relax }}{19}{figure.caption.36}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:crosscheck}{{3}{19}{Relative slippage against mark-out, both execution styles. The two quantities are computed from different data at different grains --- one from the volume-weighted prices of a whole window, the other from the mid-price after each individual fill --- so their agreement is a check on the measurement rather than an identity. They agree to within a couple of hundredths of a tick throughout.\relax }{figure.caption.36}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {8.4}Latency}{19}{subsection.8.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:latency}{{8.4}{19}{Latency}{subsection.8.4}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{1. Cost rises monotonically.}{19}{section*.38}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Latency sensitivity, the same delay on order, cancel and feed. ES futures, 246 sessions of 2025, 100 contracts bought and 100 sold per window, every other parameter fixed within each style. The configurations here are \emph  {not} the pair of Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref {tab:styles}: that pair was selected for the closest realized participation match, whereas these two were each left at their own working settings so that the delay is the only thing varied within a style. Both styles therefore sit at a different placement rate here than in Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref {tab:styles}: the aggressive one shadows $1\,\%$ of prints against $2\,\%$ there, which is why its zero-delay row reads $3.18\,\%$ participation and $92$\,s rather than $4.86\,\%$ and $62.4$\,s, and the passive one reads $4.51\,\%$ against $4.46\,\%$. Rows are comparable down a column; across tables they are not.\relax }}{20}{table.caption.37}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:latency}{{5}{20}{Latency sensitivity, the same delay on order, cancel and feed. ES futures, 246 sessions of 2025, 100 contracts bought and 100 sold per window, every other parameter fixed within each style. The configurations here are \emph {not} the pair of Table~\ref {tab:styles}: that pair was selected for the closest realized participation match, whereas these two were each left at their own working settings so that the delay is the only thing varied within a style. Both styles therefore sit at a different placement rate here than in Table~\ref {tab:styles}: the aggressive one shadows $1\,\%$ of prints against $2\,\%$ there, which is why its zero-delay row reads $3.18\,\%$ participation and $92$\,s rather than $4.86\,\%$ and $62.4$\,s, and the passive one reads $4.51\,\%$ against $4.46\,\%$. Rows are comparable down a column; across tables they are not.\relax }{table.caption.37}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{2. Neither style holds its character.}{20}{section*.39}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{3. Participation moves in opposite directions.}{20}{section*.40}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{4. The degradation is in the price, not only in the mix.}{20}{section*.41}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{5. The latency sensitivity is itself evidence that the method extracts information from the flow.}{20}{section*.42}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Relative slippage against round-trip delay --- the cost column of Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref {tab:latency} --- with 95\,\% intervals clustered by session. The same delay is applied to the outbound order, the outbound cancel and the inbound feed. The two styles start together and separate at once: aggressive POV is the cheaper of the two at zero delay and the more expensive by $500\,\mu $s, and its slope over the range is about four times the passive one.\relax }}{21}{figure.caption.43}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:latency}{{4}{21}{Relative slippage against round-trip delay --- the cost column of Table~\ref {tab:latency} --- with 95\,\% intervals clustered by session. The same delay is applied to the outbound order, the outbound cancel and the inbound feed. The two styles start together and separate at once: aggressive POV is the cheaper of the two at zero delay and the more expensive by $500\,\mu $s, and its slope over the range is about four times the passive one.\relax }{figure.caption.43}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Aggregate participation against round-trip delay, the same runs as Figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref {fig:latency}. Passive \textsc  {Shadow-PPOV}{} takes a \emph  {larger} share of the market's volume as it gets slower and aggressive POV a smaller one. Both follow from the conversion of execution style: the passive side turns quotes into crosses and finishes sooner, the aggressive side loses crosses and takes longer, and participation is scored over the leg's own duration.\relax }}{21}{figure.caption.44}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:latencypart}{{5}{21}{Aggregate participation against round-trip delay, the same runs as Figure~\ref {fig:latency}. Passive \pov {} takes a \emph {larger} share of the market's volume as it gets slower and aggressive POV a smaller one. Both follow from the conversion of execution style: the passive side turns quotes into crosses and finishes sooner, the aggressive side loses crosses and takes longer, and participation is scored over the leg's own duration.\relax }{figure.caption.44}{}}
